STORIES_LOAD("2kgs",{"m2a":{"passage":[[1,2,4],[1,9,17]],"intro":[{"sc":"throne","n":"King Ahaziah, Ahab's son, fell through the lattice of an upstairs room and was badly hurt. He sent messengers to ask Baal-zebub, the idol of Ekron, whether he'd get better."},{"sc":"wilderness","n":"Elijah met the messengers: \"Is there no God in Israel, that you go to ask Baal-zebub? Tell the king he will not get up from his bed.\""},{"sc":"carmel","n":"The king sent a captain with fifty soldiers to arrest Elijah. \"Man of God, the king says come down!\" Elijah said, \"If I'm a man of God, let fire come down and burn you up!\" And fire fell. A second captain with fifty came. The same thing happened."},{"sc":"carmel","n":"The third captain fell on his knees. \"Man of God, please spare my life and the lives of these fifty men!\" The angel said to Elijah, \"Go with him. Don't be afraid.\""},{"sc":"throne","n":"Elijah went to the king and told him the same message to his face. And Ahaziah died, as the Lord had said."}],"lesson":{"title":"Humility makes the difference","text":"Two captains came with orders and arrogance. The third came on his knees, asking for mercy, and he and his men lived. Also notice the king's sin: going to an idol instead of God. Leaders seek God first, and approach Him humbly."},"order":["King Ahaziah falls and is hurt","He sends messengers to an idol","Elijah stops the messengers","Fire falls on two captains and their men","The third captain begs for mercy","Elijah tells the king he will die"],"quiz":[{"q":"Who did the king ask about getting better?","o":["Baal-zebub, an idol","Elijah","God","A doctor"],"why":"2 Kings 1:2."},{"q":"What happened to the first two captains?","o":["Fire came down on them","They ran away","They became prophets","They got lost"],"why":"2 Kings 1:10-12."},{"q":"Why was the third captain spared?","o":["He humbly begged for mercy","He was strong","He brought gold","He was a priest"],"why":"2 Kings 1:13."}],"words":["ELIJAH","FIRE","CAPTAIN","FIFTY","HUMBLE","IDOL"]},"m2b":{"passage":[[2,1,15]],"intro":[{"sc":"jordan","n":"The day came for God to take Elijah up to heaven. Elijah kept telling Elisha, \"Stay here.\" Three times! And three times Elisha said, \"As the Lord lives, I will not leave you!\""},{"sc":"jordan","n":"At the Jordan river, Elijah rolled up his cloak and struck the water. It parted, and the two of them walked across on dry ground."},{"sc":"jordan","w":"belijah","e":"smile","t":"Tell me, what can I do for you before I'm taken from you?"},{"sc":"jordan","w":"belisha","e":"worried","t":"Please, let me receive a double portion of your spirit."},{"sc":"whirlwind","n":"As they walked and talked, suddenly a chariot of fire and horses of fire swept between them, and Elijah went up to heaven in a whirlwind! Elisha cried, \"My father! My father! The chariots and horsemen of Israel!\""},{"sc":"jordan","n":"Elijah's cloak had fallen. Elisha picked it up, walked back to the Jordan, and struck the water: \"Where is the Lord, the God of Elijah?\" The water parted! The prophets watching said, \"The spirit of Elijah rests on Elisha.\""}],"lesson":{"title":"Stay close, ask big, then step up","text":"Elisha refused to leave his mentor, three times. He asked for something big. And when Elijah was gone, he didn't sit and cry; he picked up the cloak and stepped into the river himself. Leaders stay close to their mentors, ask boldly, and step up when it's their turn."},"order":["Elisha refuses to leave Elijah three times","Elijah parts the Jordan with his cloak","Elisha asks for a double portion","A chariot of fire appears","Elijah goes up in a whirlwind","Elisha parts the Jordan with the cloak"],"quiz":[{"q":"What did Elisha ask for?","o":["A double portion of Elijah's spirit","Elijah's house","Money","A chariot"],"why":"2 Kings 2:9."},{"q":"How did Elijah go to heaven?","o":["In a whirlwind, with a chariot of fire","He died in bed","On a mountain","In a boat"],"why":"2 Kings 2:11."},{"q":"What did Elisha say as he struck the water?","o":["\"Where is the Lord, the God of Elijah?\"","\"Part!\"","\"Help me!\"","Nothing"],"why":"2 Kings 2:14."}],"words":["CHARIOT","WHIRLWIND","CLOAK","DOUBLE","ELISHA","JORDAN"]},"m2c":{"passage":[[2,19,25]],"intro":[{"sc":"citygate","n":"The people of Jericho told Elisha: \"Our city is pleasant, but the water is bad, and the land can't grow anything.\""},{"sc":"citygate","n":"Elisha asked for a new bowl with salt in it. He threw the salt into the spring: \"The Lord says, 'I have healed this water.'\" And the water stayed pure from then on!"},{"sc":"woods","n":"As Elisha walked up the road to Bethel, a large gang of young men came out of the city and jeered at God's prophet: \"Go on up, baldhead! Go on up, baldhead!\" They were mocking him, and mocking the God who had just taken Elijah up."},{"sc":"woods","n":"Elisha turned and cursed them in the name of the Lord. Two bears came out of the woods and mauled forty-two of them."},{"sc":"woods","n":"It's a hard story. It shows that mocking God and His messengers is serious, especially in a city like Bethel, the centre of Israel's idol worship."}],"lesson":{"title":"Respect God's people and God's name","text":"One story shows God healing a whole city's water. The other shows a gang mocking God's prophet and the God he served. Mocking God is never a small thing. Leaders show respect, especially to people who serve God."},"order":["Jericho's water is bad","Elisha throws salt into the spring","The water becomes pure","A gang mocks Elisha","They call him 'baldhead'","Two bears come out of the woods"],"quiz":[{"q":"What did Elisha throw into the spring?","o":["Salt","Sugar","A stick","Oil"],"why":"2 Kings 2:21."},{"q":"What did the gang shout at Elisha?","o":["\"Go on up, baldhead!\"","\"Welcome!\"","\"Help us!\"","\"Where is Elijah?\""],"why":"2 Kings 2:23."},{"q":"What came out of the woods?","o":["Two bears","Lions","Wolves","Soldiers"],"why":"2 Kings 2:24."}],"words":["SALT","WATER","JERICHO","BEARS","RESPECT","BALDHEAD"]},"m2d":{"passage":[[3,9,24]],"intro":[{"sc":"wilderness","n":"Three kings marched together against Moab: the kings of Israel, Judah and Edom. After seven days in the desert, there was no water left for the army or the animals."},{"sc":"wilderness","n":"Good King Jehoshaphat said, \"Is there a prophet of the Lord here?\" They went to Elisha."},{"sc":"wilderness","w":"belisha","e":"stern","t":"The Lord says: dig this valley full of ditches! You won't see wind or rain, but this valley will be filled with water."},{"sc":"wilderness","w":"belisha","e":"smile","t":"And that's an easy thing for the Lord. He'll give you Moab too!"},{"sc":"wilderness","n":"The next morning, water came flowing from the direction of Edom, and filled every ditch! When the Moabites saw the sun shining on the water, it looked red like blood. \"The kings have fought each other! Let's grab the spoil!\" They charged in, and were defeated."}],"lesson":{"title":"Dig the ditches, expect the rain","text":"God told them to dig ditches for water, with no clouds in sight. Digging was an act of faith: preparing for what God promised before seeing it. Leaders prepare for God's answers even before they arrive."},"order":["Three kings march against Moab","The army runs out of water","They ask Elisha","Elisha says dig ditches","Water fills the ditches by morning","Moab thinks the water is blood"],"quiz":[{"q":"What did Elisha tell them to dig?","o":["Ditches","Wells","Graves","Tunnels"],"why":"2 Kings 3:16."},{"q":"Did it rain?","o":["No, but water came anyway","Yes, a storm","Snow fell","Only a little"],"why":"2 Kings 3:17."},{"q":"What did the water look like to Moab?","o":["Red like blood","Blue","Gold","Like milk"],"why":"2 Kings 3:22."}],"words":["DITCHES","WATER","MOAB","DIG","FAITH","PREPARE"]},"m2e":{"passage":[[4,1,7]],"intro":[{"sc":"hut","n":"The wife of one of the prophets came crying to Elisha. \"My husband is dead, and you know he feared the Lord. Now the man he owed money to is coming to take my two sons as slaves!\""},{"sc":"hut","w":"belisha","e":"smile","t":"How can I help you? Tell me, what do you have in your house?"},{"sc":"hut","n":"\"Nothing, except a small jar of oil.\" \"Go round to all your neighbours and borrow empty jars. Lots of them! Not just a few.\""},{"sc":"jars","n":"\"Then go inside with your sons, shut the door, and pour the oil into all the jars.\" She poured, and poured, and the oil kept flowing! Jar after jar filled up!"},{"sc":"jars","n":"\"Bring me another jar,\" she told her son. \"There aren't any more.\" And the oil stopped. Elisha said, \"Go sell the oil and pay your debts. You and your sons can live on the rest.\""}],"lesson":{"title":"God uses what you have","text":"Elisha asked, 'What do you have?' She had almost nothing, just a little oil. God multiplied it, and the only limit was how many jars she gathered. Bring what little you have to God, and make room for Him to fill."},"order":["A widow's sons are about to be taken","Elisha asks what she has","She has a little oil","She borrows many empty jars","The oil fills every jar","She pays her debts"],"quiz":[{"q":"What did the widow have in her house?","o":["A small jar of oil","Gold","Bread","Nothing at all"],"why":"2 Kings 4:2."},{"q":"What did Elisha tell her to borrow?","o":["Empty jars","Money","Oil","Bread"],"why":"2 Kings 4:3."},{"q":"When did the oil stop?","o":["When there were no more jars","After one jar","Never","At midnight"],"why":"2 Kings 4:6."}],"words":["OIL","JARS","WIDOW","POUR","DEBTS","PLENTY"]},"m2f":{"passage":[[4,8,17],[4,18,37]],"intro":[{"sc":"hut","n":"In the town of Shunem lived a wealthy woman. Whenever Elisha passed by, she invited him for a meal."},{"sc":"hut","w":"bshunammite","e":"smile","t":"This man is a holy man of God. Let's build him a little room on the roof, with a bed, a table, a chair and a lamp, so he can stay whenever he comes."},{"sc":"upperroom","n":"Elisha wanted to thank her. His servant Gehazi said, \"She has no son.\" Elisha promised: \"This time next year, you'll hold a son in your arms.\" And she did!"},{"sc":"ploughfield","n":"Years later, the boy was out in the fields with his father at harvest. \"My head! My head!\" He was carried home, sat on his mother's lap until noon, and died."},{"sc":"upperroom","n":"She laid him on Elisha's bed, shut the door, and rode straight to Elisha. When Gehazi asked, \"Is everything well?\" she said, \"All is well.\" But to Elisha, she grabbed his feet and wouldn't let go."},{"sc":"upperroom","n":"Elisha came, prayed, and stretched himself over the boy. The boy sneezed seven times and opened his eyes! \"Pick up your son,\" Elisha said."}],"lesson":{"title":"Generosity and stubborn faith","text":"She built a room for God's servant, expecting nothing back. When tragedy came, she didn't give up or spread panic. She went straight to the one who could help and wouldn't let go. Leaders are generous, and they hold on to God in the hardest moments."},"order":["The woman feeds Elisha","She builds him a room on the roof","Elisha promises her a son","The boy dies at harvest","She rides to Elisha","The boy sneezes seven times and lives"],"quiz":[{"q":"What did the woman build for Elisha?","o":["A room on her roof","A temple","A boat","A tower"],"why":"2 Kings 4:10."},{"q":"What did she say when asked if everything was well?","o":["\"All is well\"","\"Help!\"","\"My son is dead\"","Nothing"],"why":"2 Kings 4:26."},{"q":"How many times did the boy sneeze?","o":["Seven","Once","Three","Twelve"],"why":"2 Kings 4:35."}],"words":["SHUNEM","ROOM","ROOF","SON","SNEEZE","FAITH"]},"m2g":{"passage":[[4,38,44]],"intro":[{"sc":"kitchen","n":"There was a famine. Elisha was teaching a group of young prophets. \"Put on the big pot and make stew.\""},{"sc":"kitchen","n":"One of them went out to pick herbs. He found a wild vine with gourds, filled his cloak, and chopped them into the stew, not knowing they were poisonous."},{"sc":"kitchen","n":"As they started eating, they cried out, \"Man of God, there's death in the pot!\" Elisha said, \"Bring some flour.\" He threw it in. \"Serve it.\" And now there was nothing harmful in the pot!"},{"sc":"barley","n":"Another day, a man brought Elisha twenty small barley loaves from his first harvest. \"Give it to the people to eat.\""},{"sc":"barley","n":"\"How can I feed a hundred men with this?\" his servant asked. Elisha said: \"The Lord says, 'They will eat and have some left over.'\" And they did, just as the Lord had said."}],"lesson":{"title":"God turns not enough into more than enough","text":"Poison in the pot, and not enough bread for a crowd. Both times, God made a way. Hundreds of years later, Jesus did something similar with five loaves. Leaders bring their 'not enough' to God and watch Him provide."},"order":["A famine comes","A young prophet picks wild gourds","The stew is poisonous","Elisha throws in flour","A man brings twenty loaves","A hundred men eat with some left over"],"quiz":[{"q":"What did the young prophets cry out?","o":["\"There's death in the pot!\"","\"It's delicious!\"","\"More please!\"","\"Where's the bread?\""],"why":"2 Kings 4:40."},{"q":"What did Elisha throw into the pot?","o":["Flour","Salt","Water","Honey"],"why":"2 Kings 4:41."},{"q":"How many men did twenty loaves feed?","o":["A hundred, with some left over","Ten","Twenty","Five thousand"],"why":"2 Kings 4:43-44."}],"words":["POT","STEW","FLOUR","LOAVES","HUNDRED","PLENTY"]},"m2h":{"passage":[[5,1,19]],"intro":[{"sc":"throne","n":"Naaman was the commander of the army of Aram, a great and brave man. But he had a terrible skin disease."},{"sc":"throne","n":"Aramean raiders had captured a little Israelite girl, and she became a servant to Naaman's wife."},{"sc":"throne","w":"bmaid","e":"smile","t":"If only my master would go to the prophet in Samaria! He would heal him!"},{"sc":"hut","n":"Naaman came with horses, chariots and treasure to Elisha's door. Elisha didn't even come out. He sent a messenger: \"Go wash seven times in the Jordan river, and you'll be healed.\""},{"sc":"hut","w":"bnaaman","e":"frown","t":"I thought he'd come out and wave his hand over me! Aren't the rivers back home better than all the waters of Israel? Why should I wash in this muddy one?"},{"sc":"jordan","n":"His servants said, \"If he'd asked you to do something hard, wouldn't you have done it? He just said 'Wash!'\" So Naaman went down and dipped himself in the Jordan seven times..."},{"sc":"jordan","n":"...and his skin became clean, like the skin of a little child! He went back to Elisha: \"Now I know there is no God in all the world except in Israel!\""}],"lesson":{"title":"Humility opens the door","text":"A brave, important general needed a captured little girl's words, and a simple, humbling instruction. His pride almost made him miss his healing. And the girl, far from home, chose to help instead of resent. Leaders listen to small voices and humble themselves."},"order":["A little girl is captured and serves Naaman's wife","She tells them about Elisha","Naaman goes to Elisha's door","Elisha says wash seven times in the Jordan","Naaman is angry","He washes and his skin is healed"],"quiz":[{"q":"Who told Naaman's family about Elisha?","o":["A little captured servant girl","The king","A soldier","Gehazi"],"why":"2 Kings 5:3."},{"q":"How many times did Naaman dip in the Jordan?","o":["Seven","Once","Three","Twelve"],"why":"2 Kings 5:14."},{"q":"Why was Naaman angry at first?","o":["He expected something grand, not a muddy river","He was cold","He was tired","He lost his horse"],"why":"2 Kings 5:11-12."}],"words":["NAAMAN","JORDAN","SEVEN","GIRL","HUMBLE","HEALED"]},"m2i":{"passage":[[5,15,16],[5,20,27]],"intro":[{"sc":"hut","n":"Naaman tried to give Elisha a huge gift of silver, gold and fine clothes. Elisha refused: \"As the Lord lives, I won't accept anything.\""},{"sc":"wilderness","n":"But Elisha's servant Gehazi thought: \"My master let him off too easily! I'll run after him and get something.\""},{"sc":"wilderness","w":"bgehazi","e":"smile","t":"My master sent me. Two young prophets just arrived. Please give them some silver and two sets of clothes."},{"sc":"hut","n":"Naaman gladly gave him double. Gehazi hid it all in his house, then walked in to Elisha as if nothing had happened."},{"sc":"hut","w":"belisha","e":"stern","t":"Where have you been, Gehazi?"},{"sc":"hut","w":"bgehazi","e":"worried","t":"Nowhere, my lord."},{"sc":"hut","w":"belisha","e":"stern","t":"Wasn't my spirit with you when the man got down from his chariot to meet you? Is this the time to take money and clothes?"},{"sc":"hut","n":"Naaman's skin disease came upon Gehazi. He walked out from Elisha's presence, his skin white as snow."}],"lesson":{"title":"Greed leads to lies","text":"Elisha refused payment so Naaman would know God's healing was free. Gehazi saw a chance to get rich, and told one lie, then another. Greed and lies always come to light. Leaders guard against greed, especially when no one seems to be watching."},"order":["Elisha refuses Naaman's gifts","Gehazi runs after Naaman","Gehazi lies to get silver and clothes","He hides them at home","He lies to Elisha","Naaman's disease comes upon Gehazi"],"quiz":[{"q":"Why did Elisha refuse Naaman's gifts?","o":["God's healing was free","He was rich","He didn't like Naaman","The gifts were fake"],"why":"2 Kings 5:16."},{"q":"What did Gehazi ask Naaman for?","o":["Silver and clothes","A chariot","A horse","Food"],"why":"2 Kings 5:22."},{"q":"How did Elisha know what Gehazi did?","o":["His spirit was there with him","Someone told him","He followed him","He guessed"],"why":"2 Kings 5:26."}],"words":["GEHAZI","GREED","SILVER","LIES","CLOTHES","TRUTH"]},"m2j":{"passage":[[6,1,7]],"intro":[{"sc":"jordan","n":"The group of young prophets had grown too big for their meeting place. \"Let's go to the Jordan, cut down trees, and build a bigger place.\""},{"sc":"jordan","n":"Elisha went with them. As one young man was chopping down a tree, his iron axe head flew off and splashed into the river!"},{"sc":"jordan","w":"bservant","e":"worried","t":"Oh no, master! It was borrowed!"},{"sc":"jordan","n":"In those days, iron tools were very expensive. Losing a borrowed one was a big problem. Elisha asked, \"Where did it fall?\""},{"sc":"jordan","n":"Elisha cut a stick and threw it into the water at that spot... and the iron axe head floated up to the surface! \"Pick it up,\" said Elisha. And the young man reached out and grabbed it."}],"lesson":{"title":"God cares about small problems too","text":"Not every miracle is about kings and armies. A young man lost a borrowed tool, and he cared about returning it. God cared too. Bring your small worries to God, and be honest about things you've borrowed."},"order":["The prophets need a bigger place","They go to the Jordan to cut trees","An axe head falls into the water","It was borrowed","Elisha throws a stick in","The axe head floats"],"quiz":[{"q":"What fell into the river?","o":["An iron axe head","A sword","A coin","A sandal"],"why":"2 Kings 6:5."},{"q":"Why was the young man so worried?","o":["It was borrowed","He loved it","It was gold","He was cold"],"why":"2 Kings 6:5."},{"q":"What made the axe head float?","o":["Elisha threw a stick in, and God made it float","A magnet","A fish","Nobody knows"],"why":"2 Kings 6:6."}],"words":["AXE","FLOAT","BORROWED","JORDAN","STICK","IRON"]},"m2k":{"passage":[[6,8,23]],"intro":[{"sc":"throne","n":"The king of Aram kept planning secret ambushes against Israel. But every time, Elisha warned Israel's king exactly where the enemy would be!"},{"sc":"throne","n":"\"Who's the spy?\" the king of Aram raged. \"No one, my lord. Elisha the prophet tells the king of Israel the words you speak in your bedroom!\""},{"sc":"dothan","n":"So the king sent a huge army with horses and chariots to surround the town of Dothan by night, to capture Elisha."},{"sc":"dothan","w":"bservant","e":"shock","t":"Oh no, master! What shall we do?"},{"sc":"dothan","w":"belisha","e":"smile","t":"Don't be afraid. Those who are with us are more than those who are with them."},{"sc":"dothan","n":"Elisha prayed: \"Lord, open his eyes so he can see.\" The servant looked, and the hills were full of horses and chariots of fire all around Elisha!"},{"sc":"dothan","n":"Then Elisha prayed for the enemy to be blinded, and led the whole army into Samaria. Instead of killing them, he told the king: \"Give them a feast and send them home.\" After that, the raiders stopped coming."}],"lesson":{"title":"You're never outnumbered with God","text":"The servant saw only the enemy army. Elisha saw God's army, bigger and brighter. And then Elisha surprised everyone by feeding his enemies instead of destroying them. Leaders see with eyes of faith, and they can turn enemies into friends with kindness."},"order":["Elisha warns Israel about ambushes","The king of Aram sends an army","The army surrounds Dothan","Elisha's servant is terrified","The servant sees chariots of fire","Elisha feeds the enemy and sends them home"],"quiz":[{"q":"How did Israel's king always know about the ambushes?","o":["Elisha told him","A spy","A bird","A dream"],"why":"2 Kings 6:12."},{"q":"What did the servant see when his eyes were opened?","o":["Hills full of horses and chariots of fire","Angels singing","A rainbow","Nothing"],"why":"2 Kings 6:17."},{"q":"What did Elisha do with the captured army?","o":["Gave them a feast and sent them home","Killed them","Made them slaves","Locked them up"],"why":"2 Kings 6:22-23."}],"words":["DOTHAN","CHARIOTS","FIRE","EYES","FEAST","ENEMY"]},"m2l":{"passage":[[6,24,25],[7,1,16]],"intro":[{"sc":"siege","n":"The Aramean army surrounded Samaria. Nobody could get in or out. The famine became so terrible that a donkey's head sold for eighty pieces of silver, and people did unthinkable things to survive."},{"sc":"siege","w":"belisha","e":"stern","t":"The Lord says: by this time tomorrow, flour and barley will be cheap at the city gate!"},{"sc":"siege","n":"An officer laughed: \"Even if God opened windows in heaven, could this happen?\" Elisha said: \"You'll see it with your own eyes, but you won't eat any of it.\""},{"sc":"siege","n":"Outside the city gate sat four men with leprosy, starving. \"Why sit here until we die? If we go into the city, we'll starve. If we go to the enemy camp, the worst they can do is kill us.\""},{"sc":"assyrian","n":"At dusk they crept into the enemy camp. It was empty! God had made the Arameans hear the sound of a huge army with chariots and horses, and they ran away in panic, leaving everything behind!"},{"sc":"assyrian","w":"bleper","e":"big","t":"We're not doing right. This is a day of good news, and we're keeping it to ourselves! Let's go and tell the king's household!"},{"sc":"citygate","n":"The people rushed out and the city was saved. Flour and barley were sold cheaply at the gate, exactly as Elisha said. And the officer who laughed was trampled in the rush at the gate."}],"lesson":{"title":"Don't keep good news to yourself","text":"Four outcasts found more food than they could eat. Then they realised: it's wrong to keep good news to ourselves while others starve. That's exactly how Christians think about the good news of Jesus. Leaders share what they've found."},"order":["Samaria is surrounded and starving","Elisha promises food by tomorrow","An officer laughs","Four lepers go to the enemy camp","The camp is empty","The lepers share the good news"],"quiz":[{"q":"Who discovered the empty enemy camp?","o":["Four men with leprosy","The king","Elisha","Soldiers"],"why":"2 Kings 7:3-5."},{"q":"Why did the Arameans run away?","o":["God made them hear a huge army","They were hungry","It rained","Their king called them home"],"why":"2 Kings 7:6."},{"q":"What did the lepers say they shouldn't do?","o":["Keep good news to themselves","Eat too much","Go back","Tell anyone"],"why":"2 Kings 7:9."}],"words":["LEPERS","GOOD","NEWS","SHARE","CAMP","FAMINE"]},"m2m":{"passage":[[8,1,6]],"intro":[{"sc":"hut","n":"Elisha warned the Shunammite woman: \"A seven-year famine is coming. Take your family and go live somewhere else.\" She obeyed, and lived in the land of the Philistines for seven years."},{"sc":"throne","n":"When she came home, someone else had taken her house and land. She went to the king to ask for them back."},{"sc":"throne","n":"At that very moment, the king was talking with Gehazi. \"Tell me all the great things Elisha has done.\" Gehazi was telling him about the boy who was raised from the dead..."},{"sc":"throne","n":"...and right then, the woman walked in! \"My lord the king, this is the woman, and this is her son, whom Elisha raised to life!\""},{"sc":"throne","n":"The king asked her, and she told him everything. He appointed an officer: \"Give back everything that was hers, and all the crops from her fields since the day she left!\""}],"lesson":{"title":"God's perfect timing","text":"She walked in at the exact moment the king was hearing her story. That's no coincidence. When we obey God, like she did when she left in the famine, He works out the timing. Leaders trust God's timing."},"order":["Elisha warns of a seven-year famine","The woman moves away","She comes back to find her land taken","She goes to the king","Gehazi is telling the king her story","The king gives everything back"],"quiz":[{"q":"How long was the famine?","o":["Seven years","One year","Forty days","Three years"],"why":"2 Kings 8:1."},{"q":"What was Gehazi telling the king about when she walked in?","o":["Her son being raised to life","Naaman","The axe head","Elijah"],"why":"2 Kings 8:5."},{"q":"What did the king give back?","o":["Her land and all its crops","Only her house","Nothing","Gold"],"why":"2 Kings 8:6."}],"words":["TIMING","FAMINE","LAND","KING","RESTORED","OBEY"]},"m2n":{"passage":[[8,7,15]],"intro":[{"sc":"throne","n":"King Ben-hadad of Aram was sick. He sent his officer Hazael with forty camel-loads of gifts to ask Elisha if he would recover."},{"sc":"throne","n":"Elisha looked at Hazael, staring at him so long that Hazael felt uncomfortable. Then the man of God began to weep."},{"sc":"throne","n":"\"Why is my lord weeping?\" Hazael asked. \"Because I know the terrible harm you will do to the people of Israel. You will burn their cities and kill their young people.\""},{"sc":"throne","n":"Hazael said, \"How could I, a nobody, do such a thing?\" Elisha said, \"The Lord has shown me that you will be king of Aram.\""},{"sc":"throne","n":"The very next day, Hazael killed the king and took his throne."}],"lesson":{"title":"The heart can hide terrible things","text":"Hazael acted shocked, as if he could never do such evil. But it was already in his heart. Elisha wept because he cared about the people who would suffer. Leaders care deeply about people, and stay honest about the evil the human heart is capable of."},"order":["Ben-hadad is sick","Hazael brings forty camel-loads of gifts","Elisha stares at Hazael","Elisha begins to weep","He foretells the harm Hazael will do","Hazael kills the king"],"quiz":[{"q":"Why did Elisha weep?","o":["He saw the harm Hazael would do to Israel","He was sick","He was sad about Elijah","He was tired"],"why":"2 Kings 8:12."},{"q":"How many camel-loads of gifts did Hazael bring?","o":["Forty","One","Seven","A hundred"],"why":"2 Kings 8:9."},{"q":"What did Hazael do the next day?","o":["Killed the king and took the throne","Went home","Became a prophet","Ran away"],"why":"2 Kings 8:15."}],"words":["HAZAEL","WEEP","HEART","ARAM","CAMELS","KING"]},"m2o":{"passage":[[9,1,13],[9,17,24],[9,30,37]],"intro":[{"sc":"nightcamp","n":"Elisha sent a young prophet with a flask of oil to the army camp. He took the commander, Jehu, into an inner room, poured oil on his head, said \"The Lord anoints you king over Israel! You will strike down the house of Ahab\", and ran away!"},{"sc":"chariot","n":"Jehu jumped into his chariot and raced towards the city of Jezreel."},{"sc":"tower","n":"The watchman on the tower saw a cloud of dust. He sent riders out. They didn't come back. \"The driving is like the driving of Jehu! He drives like a madman!\""},{"sc":"vineyard","n":"King Joram rode out to meet him, at Naboth's vineyard of all places. \"Is it peace, Jehu?\" \"How can there be peace while Jezebel's idols and witchcraft are everywhere?\" Jehu shot an arrow, and Joram fell, right in Naboth's field."},{"sc":"tower","n":"Jezebel heard Jehu was coming. She painted her eyes, fixed her hair, and looked out of an upstairs window, mocking him."},{"sc":"tower","w":"bjehu","e":"stern","t":"Who is on my side? Who?"},{"sc":"tower","n":"Two or three servants looked out. \"Throw her down!\" They threw her down from the window. When Jehu's men later went to bury her, there was almost nothing left, just as Elijah had said: in Jezreel the dogs would eat Jezebel. God's word came true to the last detail."}],"lesson":{"title":"God's word comes true","text":"Years earlier, Elijah had said what would happen to Ahab's family and to Jezebel, in the very place where they killed Naboth. It all came true. Evil may seem to win for a while, but God sees, and His justice comes. Leaders remember God keeps every word."},"order":["A young prophet anoints Jehu","Jehu races to Jezreel","The watchman says he drives like a madman","King Joram falls in Naboth's field","Jezebel mocks from a window","Jezebel is thrown down"],"quiz":[{"q":"How did Jehu drive his chariot?","o":["Like a madman","Very slowly","Backwards","With no horses"],"why":"2 Kings 9:20."},{"q":"Where did King Joram fall?","o":["Naboth's vineyard","In battle","At the palace","At the Jordan"],"why":"2 Kings 9:25."},{"q":"What did Jehu shout up to the window?","o":["\"Who is on my side?\"","\"Come down!\"","\"Peace!\"","\"Goodbye\""],"why":"2 Kings 9:32."}],"words":["JEHU","CHARIOT","JEZEBEL","WINDOW","JUSTICE","JEZREEL"]},"m2p":{"passage":[[10,18,31]],"intro":[{"sc":"citygate","w":"bjehu","e":"smirk","t":"Ahab served Baal a little. Jehu will serve him much! Call all of Baal's prophets and priests and worshippers. I'm holding a great sacrifice for Baal. Anyone who doesn't come will die!"},{"sc":"temple","n":"Every single Baal worshipper in Israel came. The temple of Baal was packed from one end to the other. Jehu gave them all special robes."},{"sc":"temple","n":"Jehu checked carefully that no worshippers of the Lord were inside. Then he posted eighty soldiers outside."},{"sc":"temple","n":"When the sacrifice was done, Jehu's soldiers went in and struck down the worshippers of Baal. They broke down the sacred stone of Baal and destroyed his temple. It became a public toilet!"},{"sc":"throne","n":"Jehu wiped out Baal worship from Israel. But the Bible gives a sad verdict: \"Jehu was not careful to follow the law of the Lord with all his heart.\" He kept Jeroboam's golden calves."}],"lesson":{"title":"Zeal isn't the same as a whole heart","text":"Jehu was fierce and effective against Baal. But he kept the golden calves that served his political interests. You can be passionate about fighting some wrongs while ignoring your own. Leaders follow God with ALL their heart, not just the parts that suit them."},"order":["Jehu pretends to serve Baal","All Baal's worshippers gather","Jehu checks no true worshippers are inside","His soldiers strike down the worshippers","Baal's temple becomes a toilet","Jehu keeps the golden calves"],"quiz":[{"q":"How did Jehu gather all the Baal worshippers?","o":["He pretended to hold a huge sacrifice for Baal","He sent invitations to a party","He arrested them","He paid them"],"why":"2 Kings 10:19."},{"q":"What did Baal's temple become?","o":["A public toilet","A market","A palace","A school"],"why":"2 Kings 10:27."},{"q":"What was the sad verdict on Jehu?","o":["He didn't follow God with all his heart","He was lazy","He loved Baal","He was a coward"],"why":"2 Kings 10:31."}],"words":["JEHU","BAAL","TRICK","TEMPLE","HEART","CALVES"]},"m2q":{"passage":[[11,1,4],[11,9,20]],"intro":[{"sc":"throne","n":"When her son the king died, Athaliah, Jezebel's daughter, decided to make herself queen of Judah. She set out to kill the entire royal family, all of David's descendants!"},{"sc":"temple","n":"But Princess Jehosheba, wife of the priest Jehoiada, grabbed her baby nephew Joash from among the princes who were being killed, and hid him and his nurse in a bedroom."},{"sc":"temple","n":"For six years, little Joash was secretly raised inside God's temple, while wicked Athaliah ruled the land, not knowing he was alive."},{"sc":"temple","n":"In the seventh year, the priest Jehoiada gathered the army commanders, showed them the boy, and set guards all around the temple with spears and shields."},{"sc":"temple","n":"Jehoiada brought out seven-year-old Joash, put the crown on his head, and anointed him king! Everyone clapped and shouted, \"Long live the king!\""},{"sc":"temple","n":"Athaliah heard the noise and rushed in, tearing her clothes: \"Treason! Treason!\" She was taken out and put to death. The people tore down the temple of Baal, and the land had peace."}],"lesson":{"title":"Brave people protect the vulnerable","text":"One brave woman hid one baby, and saved the whole royal line of David, the line Jesus would come from. A priest guarded a secret for six years. Small, hidden acts of courage can change history. Leaders protect those who can't protect themselves."},"order":["Athaliah tries to kill the royal family","Jehosheba hides baby Joash","Joash grows up hidden in the temple","Jehoiada gathers the guards","Seven-year-old Joash is crowned","Athaliah cries, \"Treason!\""],"quiz":[{"q":"Who hid baby Joash?","o":["Princess Jehosheba","Athaliah","Elisha","Jehu"],"why":"2 Kings 11:2."},{"q":"Where was Joash hidden?","o":["In God's temple","In a cave","In Egypt","In a basket"],"why":"2 Kings 11:3."},{"q":"How old was Joash when he was crowned?","o":["Seven","Twelve","One","Twenty"],"why":"2 Kings 11:21."}],"words":["JOASH","HIDDEN","TEMPLE","CROWN","ATHALIAH","BRAVE"]},"m2r":{"passage":[[12,1,16]],"intro":[{"sc":"temple","n":"King Joash did what was right in God's eyes all the years that Jehoiada the priest taught him."},{"sc":"temple","n":"God's temple had been damaged and neglected. Joash told the priests to collect money and repair it. But years went by, and nothing was fixed!"},{"sc":"temple","n":"So Jehoiada took a chest, bored a hole in its lid, and set it beside the altar. People put their offerings in it."},{"sc":"temple","n":"When the chest was full, the king's secretary and the high priest counted the money together, and paid it straight to the carpenters, builders and stonemasons."},{"sc":"temple","n":"They didn't even need to check the workers' accounts, because they were completely honest. And the temple was repaired."}],"lesson":{"title":"Make it easy to give, and be honest","text":"The first plan failed. So they made a simple, transparent system: a box, two people counting, and money going straight to the work. Honest workers didn't need checking. Leaders build trustworthy systems, and they are trustworthy themselves."},"order":["Joash does right while Jehoiada teaches him","The temple needs repairs","Years pass with nothing fixed","Jehoiada makes a money chest","Two people count the money together","Honest workers repair the temple"],"quiz":[{"q":"Who taught Joash?","o":["Jehoiada the priest","Elisha","Jehu","Athaliah"],"why":"2 Kings 12:2."},{"q":"What did Jehoiada make to collect money?","o":["A chest with a hole in its lid","A bag","A jar","A safe"],"why":"2 Kings 12:9."},{"q":"Why didn't they check the workers' accounts?","o":["The workers were completely honest","They forgot","The king said no","There was no money"],"why":"2 Kings 12:15."}],"words":["CHEST","MONEY","HONEST","REPAIR","TEMPLE","GIVE"]},"m2s":{"passage":[[13,14,21]],"intro":[{"sc":"throne","n":"Elisha became very sick, and was about to die. King Jehoash came and wept over him."},{"sc":"throne","w":"belisha","e":"stern","t":"Take a bow and arrows. Put your hand on the bow. Open the east window. Shoot!"},{"sc":"arrows","n":"The king shot. \"The Lord's arrow of victory over Aram!\" said Elisha. \"Now take the arrows and strike the ground.\" The king struck the ground three times, and stopped."},{"sc":"arrows","w":"belisha","e":"frown","t":"You should have struck five or six times! Then you would have defeated Aram completely. Now you'll only win three times."},{"sc":"tents","n":"Elisha died and was buried. Some time later, a group of men were burying someone when raiders appeared. In a panic, they threw the dead man into Elisha's tomb..."},{"sc":"tents","n":"...and when the body touched Elisha's bones, the man came back to life and stood up on his feet!"}],"lesson":{"title":"Don't stop halfway","text":"The king struck the ground only three times, a half-hearted effort, and got only partial victory. Elisha wanted him to keep going. And even after death, God's power through Elisha brought life. Leaders give their full effort, not a timid half."},"order":["Elisha becomes very sick","The king shoots an arrow from the window","Elisha tells him to strike the ground","The king strikes only three times","Elisha dies","A dead man touches Elisha's bones and lives"],"quiz":[{"q":"How many times did the king strike the ground?","o":["Three","Five","Seven","Once"],"why":"2 Kings 13:18."},{"q":"Why was Elisha angry?","o":["The king should have struck five or six times","He missed","He was rude","He broke the bow"],"why":"2 Kings 13:19."},{"q":"What happened when a dead man touched Elisha's bones?","o":["He came back to life","Nothing","The tomb shook","Fire came"],"why":"2 Kings 13:21."}],"words":["ARROWS","STRIKE","THREE","BONES","ALIVE","EFFORT"]},"m2t":{"passage":[[14,1,7],[15,1,5],[15,32,35]],"intro":[{"sc":"throne","n":"In Judah, King Amaziah did what was right, but not completely like David. He won a big victory over Edom, then got proud and picked a fight with Israel, and lost badly."},{"sc":"throne","n":"His son Azariah (also called Uzziah) became king at sixteen and reigned 52 years! He did right, but he became proud and tried to burn incense in the temple, which only priests could do. He was struck with a skin disease, and lived in a separate house until he died."},{"sc":"throne","n":"In Israel, King Jeroboam the Second won back lots of land, just as the prophet Jonah had said. Yes, that Jonah!"},{"sc":"ruinsfire","n":"But Israel's kings kept murdering each other: Zechariah lasted six months, Shallum one month! Kings rose and fell like waves, and the nation weakened."},{"sc":"throne","n":"In Judah, Jotham did right and built the Upper Gate of the temple. But his son Ahaz worshipped idols, and even copied a foreign altar for God's temple."}],"lesson":{"title":"Pride ruins good starts","text":"Amaziah and Uzziah both started well, and both were brought down by pride after success. Success is a test. Leaders stay humble when they win, and know their limits."},"order":["Amaziah wins, gets proud, then loses","Uzziah becomes king at sixteen","Uzziah is struck for his pride","Jeroboam II wins back land, as Jonah said","Israel's kings murder each other","Ahaz worships idols"],"quiz":[{"q":"How old was Uzziah when he became king?","o":["Sixteen","Seven","Thirty","Eight"],"why":"2 Kings 15:2."},{"q":"Which famous prophet is mentioned here?","o":["Jonah","Daniel","Moses","Samuel"],"why":"2 Kings 14:25."},{"q":"What brought down both Amaziah and Uzziah?","o":["Pride after success","Laziness","Enemies","Sickness only"],"why":"2 Chronicles 26:16."}],"words":["UZZIAH","AMAZIAH","JONAH","PRIDE","KINGS","JOTHAM"]},"m2u":{"passage":[[17,5,18]],"intro":[{"sc":"throne","n":"God had warned Israel, the northern kingdom, again and again, for two hundred years. Prophet after prophet said: \"Turn from your evil ways! Keep My commands!\""},{"sc":"calf","n":"But they wouldn't listen. They kept the golden calves. They worshipped Baal and the stars. They followed the nations around them and even sacrificed their children in fire."},{"sc":"assyrian","n":"So in the ninth year of King Hoshea, the mighty Assyrian army besieged the capital, Samaria, for three years."},{"sc":"ruinsfire","n":"Samaria fell. The Assyrians carried the people of Israel away to distant lands. The ten northern tribes were scattered, and never came back as a nation."},{"sc":"ruinsfire","n":"The Bible explains why, sadly and clearly: \"They would not listen. They rejected His laws and the warnings He gave them.\" Only the tribe of Judah was left."}],"lesson":{"title":"Warnings are a gift","text":"God didn't destroy Israel suddenly. He sent warning after warning for two hundred years. Ignoring good warnings again and again leads to disaster. Leaders listen to warnings early, and change course while there's still time."},"order":["God warns Israel through many prophets","Israel keeps worshipping idols","Assyria besieges Samaria","Samaria falls after three years","The people are carried away","Only Judah is left"],"quiz":[{"q":"Which empire conquered Israel?","o":["Assyria","Egypt","Babylon","Rome"],"why":"2 Kings 17:6."},{"q":"How long was Samaria besieged?","o":["Three years","Seven days","Forty years","One month"],"why":"2 Kings 17:5."},{"q":"Why did Israel fall?","o":["They refused to listen to God's warnings","They were too small","They had no army","Bad luck"],"why":"2 Kings 17:14."}],"words":["ISRAEL","ASSYRIA","SAMARIA","WARNINGS","LISTEN","SCATTERED"]},"m2v":{"passage":[[17,24,33]],"intro":[{"sc":"hillcountry","n":"The king of Assyria brought people from other nations to live in Israel's empty towns."},{"sc":"hillcountry","n":"But these new settlers didn't worship the Lord. So the Lord sent lions among them, and some were killed."},{"sc":"throne","n":"They complained to the Assyrian king: \"We don't know how the God of this land wants to be worshipped!\" So the king sent back one of the captured priests to teach them."},{"sc":"hillcountry","n":"The priest taught them about the Lord. They worshipped Him... but they ALSO kept worshipping all the gods they'd brought from home, each nation its own."},{"sc":"hillcountry","n":"The Bible says: \"They feared the Lord, but they also served their own gods.\" Even their great-grandchildren were still doing it."}],"lesson":{"title":"You can't have God on the side","text":"The settlers added God to their collection of gods, like an extra just in case. But God doesn't want to be one choice among many. Leaders give God the whole heart, not a corner of it."},"order":["Assyria brings settlers to Israel","Lions come among them","They ask how to worship the God of the land","A priest comes to teach them","They worship God and their own gods","Their descendants do the same"],"quiz":[{"q":"What did God send among the settlers?","o":["Lions","Bears","Snakes","Locusts"],"why":"2 Kings 17:25."},{"q":"Who was sent to teach them?","o":["One of the captured priests","Elisha","The king","Nobody"],"why":"2 Kings 17:28."},{"q":"What was wrong with their worship?","o":["They also kept serving their own gods","They didn't pray","They were too loud","Nothing"],"why":"2 Kings 17:33."}],"words":["LIONS","SETTLERS","PRIEST","GODS","WHOLE","HEART"]},"m2w":{"passage":[[18,1,8]],"intro":[{"sc":"temple","n":"In Judah, a new king came to the throne at 25: Hezekiah. He did what was right in the eyes of the Lord, just as David had done."},{"sc":"temple","n":"He removed the idols and broke down the sacred stones."},{"sc":"temple","n":"He also did something surprising. Remember the bronze snake Moses made in the desert, that healed people who looked at it? It had been kept for 700 years, and people had started burning incense to it, worshipping it! They even gave it a name: Nehushtan."},{"sc":"temple","n":"Hezekiah smashed it to pieces. \"It's just a piece of bronze!\""},{"sc":"temple","n":"The Bible says: \"He trusted in the Lord, the God of Israel. There was no one like him among all the kings of Judah, before him or after him.\""}],"lesson":{"title":"Don't worship good things instead of God","text":"The bronze snake was a good gift from God, once. But people started worshipping the object instead of the God who healed. Hezekiah was brave enough to destroy a precious old relic because it had become an idol. Even good things can become idols. Leaders keep God first."},"order":["Hezekiah becomes king at 25","He does what is right","He removes the idols","People worship Moses' bronze snake","Hezekiah smashes it","No king trusted God like him"],"quiz":[{"q":"How old was Hezekiah when he became king?","o":["25","7","8","16"],"why":"2 Kings 18:2."},{"q":"What old object had people started worshipping?","o":["Moses' bronze snake","The ark","Goliath's sword","Elijah's cloak"],"why":"2 Kings 18:4."},{"q":"What did Hezekiah do to it?","o":["Smashed it to pieces","Polished it","Hid it","Gave it away"],"why":"2 Kings 18:4."}],"words":["HEZEKIAH","TRUST","BRONZE","SNAKE","SMASH","IDOLS"]},"m2x":{"passage":[[18,28,36],[19,14,20],[19,32,36]],"intro":[{"sc":"assyrian","n":"The mighty king of Assyria, Sennacherib, had conquered city after city. His huge army surrounded Jerusalem."},{"sc":"assyrian","w":"brabshakeh","e":"frown","t":"Don't let Hezekiah fool you! Did any god save the other nations from the king of Assyria? How could your God save Jerusalem from my hand?"},{"sc":"assyrian","n":"He shouted it in Hebrew, so all the people on the city wall would hear and be terrified. But they stayed silent, as Hezekiah had commanded."},{"sc":"temple","n":"Then Hezekiah received a threatening letter from the king of Assyria. He went up to God's temple, and spread the letter out before the Lord."},{"sc":"temple","w":"bhezekiah","e":"worried","t":"O Lord, You alone are God over all the kingdoms of the earth. Look and see these words mocking the living God. Save us, so that everyone will know You alone are God!"},{"sc":"temple","w":"bisaiah","e":"stern","t":"The Lord says: The king of Assyria will not enter this city, or shoot an arrow here. I will defend this city."},{"sc":"assyrian","n":"That night, the angel of the Lord went out into the Assyrian camp. In the morning, 185,000 soldiers lay dead. Sennacherib went home in shame, and never came back."}],"lesson":{"title":"Spread it out before God","text":"Hezekiah didn't panic or argue with the bully. He took the threatening letter and literally spread it out before God. When something scary arrives in your life, a message, a problem, a threat, show it to God first."},"order":["Assyria surrounds Jerusalem","The commander shouts threats in Hebrew","The people stay silent","Hezekiah spreads the letter before God","Isaiah says God will defend the city","185,000 soldiers die in the night"],"quiz":[{"q":"What did Hezekiah do with the threatening letter?","o":["Spread it out before the Lord","Burned it","Wrote back","Hid it"],"why":"2 Kings 19:14."},{"q":"Which prophet gave God's answer?","o":["Isaiah","Elisha","Jonah","Samuel"],"why":"2 Kings 19:20."},{"q":"How many Assyrian soldiers died in the night?","o":["185,000","100","7,000","1 million"],"why":"2 Kings 19:35."}],"words":["LETTER","ASSYRIA","ISAIAH","PRAYER","ANGEL","DEFEND"]},"m2y":{"passage":[[20,1,11]],"intro":[{"sc":"throne","n":"Hezekiah became so sick that he was about to die. Isaiah came with a message: \"Put your house in order, for you will die.\""},{"sc":"throne","n":"Hezekiah turned his face to the wall and prayed, weeping bitterly: \"Remember, Lord, how I have walked before You faithfully, with all my heart.\""},{"sc":"throne","n":"Before Isaiah had even left the middle courtyard, God spoke to him again: \"Go back and tell Hezekiah: I have heard your prayer. I have seen your tears. I will heal you, and add fifteen years to your life.\""},{"sc":"sundial","n":"Hezekiah asked for a sign. Isaiah said: \"Should the shadow on the stairway go forward ten steps, or back ten steps?\""},{"sc":"sundial","n":"\"It's easy for a shadow to go forward. Let it go back!\" Isaiah prayed, and the Lord made the shadow go BACKWARDS ten steps on the stairway of King Ahaz!"}],"lesson":{"title":"God hears your tears","text":"Hezekiah prayed honestly and wept, and God answered before Isaiah had even left the palace. 'I have heard your prayer; I have seen your tears.' Your tears matter to God. Leaders pray honestly in hard times."},"order":["Hezekiah becomes very sick","Isaiah says he will die","Hezekiah weeps and prays","God adds fifteen years","Hezekiah asks for a sign","The shadow goes back ten steps"],"quiz":[{"q":"How many years did God add to Hezekiah's life?","o":["Fifteen","Ten","Forty","Seven"],"why":"2 Kings 20:6."},{"q":"Which way did the shadow move?","o":["Backwards ten steps","Forwards ten steps","It disappeared","It stopped"],"why":"2 Kings 20:11."},{"q":"What did God say He had seen?","o":["Hezekiah's tears","His gold","His army","His temple"],"why":"2 Kings 20:5."}],"words":["SHADOW","TEARS","FIFTEEN","HEALED","SIGN","PRAYER"]},"m2z":{"passage":[[20,12,19]],"intro":[{"sc":"throne","n":"News of Hezekiah's recovery reached the far-off land of Babylon. Its king sent messengers with letters and a gift."},{"sc":"throne","n":"Hezekiah was flattered. He showed the visitors EVERYTHING: his silver, gold, spices, precious oil, his armoury and all his treasures. There was nothing in his palace he didn't show them."},{"sc":"throne","w":"bisaiah","e":"stern","t":"What did these men see in your palace?"},{"sc":"throne","w":"bhezekiah","e":"smile","t":"They saw everything! There's nothing I didn't show them."},{"sc":"throne","w":"bisaiah","e":"stern","t":"The day will come when everything in your palace will be carried off to Babylon. Nothing will be left."}],"lesson":{"title":"Pride after victory","text":"Right after God healed him, Hezekiah used the moment to show off his riches instead of telling the visitors about God. Showing off to impress people can open doors to trouble. Leaders let their victories point to God, not to themselves."},"order":["Babylon hears of Hezekiah's recovery","Messengers bring gifts","Hezekiah shows them all his treasures","Isaiah asks what they saw","Hezekiah says everything","Isaiah says Babylon will take it all"],"quiz":[{"q":"Where did the visitors come from?","o":["Babylon","Assyria","Egypt","Sheba"],"why":"2 Kings 20:12."},{"q":"What did Hezekiah show them?","o":["Everything in his treasuries","Only the temple","Nothing","His army only"],"why":"2 Kings 20:13."},{"q":"What did Isaiah say would happen?","o":["Everything would be carried off to Babylon","Babylon would be his friend","Nothing","He would get richer"],"why":"2 Kings 20:17."}],"words":["BABYLON","TREASURE","SHOWOFF","PRIDE","ISAIAH","WARNING"]},"m2aa":{"passage":[[21,1,9],[21,16,18]],"intro":[{"sc":"throne","n":"Hezekiah's son Manasseh became king at just twelve years old, and reigned for 55 years, longer than any other king of Judah."},{"sc":"temple","n":"But he undid everything his father had done. He rebuilt the idol shrines, put altars to the stars right in God's temple, practised witchcraft and consulted mediums."},{"sc":"temple","n":"He even burned his own son in sacrifice, and filled Jerusalem with innocent blood. The Bible says he led the people to do more evil than the nations God had driven out."},{"sc":"throne","n":"God sent prophets to warn that disaster was coming to Jerusalem."},{"sc":"throne","n":"But there's a surprising ending, told in 2 Chronicles 33. Later, Manasseh was captured and taken to Babylon in chains. There, in his distress, he humbled himself greatly before God and prayed. And God heard him, and brought him home! Then Manasseh knew that the Lord is God."}],"lesson":{"title":"No one is beyond God's mercy","text":"Manasseh was the worst king Judah ever had. Yet when he finally humbled himself, God forgave him. No one is too far gone for God. But the damage he did to his nation remained. Leaders know their choices affect others for generations, and that it's never too late to turn back."},"order":["Manasseh becomes king at twelve","He rebuilds idol shrines","He puts idols in God's temple","He sheds innocent blood","He is captured and taken to Babylon","He humbles himself, and God forgives him"],"quiz":[{"q":"How long did Manasseh reign?","o":["55 years","7 days","12 years","100 years"],"why":"2 Kings 21:1."},{"q":"Whose good work did Manasseh undo?","o":["His father Hezekiah's","David's","Elijah's","Solomon's"],"why":"2 Kings 21:3."},{"q":"What happened when Manasseh humbled himself in Babylon?","o":["God heard him and brought him home","Nothing","He became a slave","He died"],"why":"2 Chronicles 33:12-13."}],"words":["MANASSEH","IDOLS","MERCY","HUMBLE","FORGIVE","TURN"]},"m2bb":{"passage":[[22,1,20]],"intro":[{"sc":"throne","n":"Josiah became king when he was only eight years old. He did what was right in the eyes of the Lord, and didn't turn aside to the right or to the left."},{"sc":"temple","n":"When he was 26, he sent his secretary Shaphan to organise repairs to the neglected temple."},{"sc":"scroll","n":"While clearing out the temple, the high priest Hilkiah found something: the Book of the Law, God's law given through Moses! It had been lost for years."},{"sc":"scroll","w":"bshaphan","e":"smile","t":"Hilkiah the priest has given me a book."},{"sc":"scroll","n":"Shaphan read it to the king. When Josiah heard God's words, and realised how far his people had wandered, he tore his clothes in grief."},{"sc":"scroll","n":"Josiah sent his officials to ask God, and they went to Huldah the prophetess. She said: judgement would come on Jerusalem, but because Josiah's heart was tender and he humbled himself, it would not come in his lifetime."}],"lesson":{"title":"Rediscover God's word","text":"God's word had been lost in God's own house! When Josiah heard it, it broke his heart and changed his life. When did you last really read God's word for yourself? Leaders keep God's word close, and let it shape them."},"order":["Josiah becomes king at eight","He orders repairs to the temple","The lost Book of the Law is found","Shaphan reads it to the king","Josiah tears his clothes","Huldah the prophetess gives God's message"],"quiz":[{"q":"How old was Josiah when he became king?","o":["Eight","Twelve","Twenty","Seven"],"why":"2 Kings 22:1."},{"q":"What was found in the temple?","o":["The lost Book of the Law","Gold","The ark","A sword"],"why":"2 Kings 22:8."},{"q":"Whom did the officials ask for God's message?","o":["Huldah the prophetess","Isaiah","Elisha","Jeremiah"],"why":"2 Kings 22:14."}],"words":["JOSIAH","BOOK","LAW","FOUND","HULDAH","TENDER"]},"m2cc":{"passage":[[23,1,3],[23,4,6],[23,21,25]],"intro":[{"sc":"temple","n":"King Josiah gathered all the people of Judah, from the least to the greatest, at the temple. He read the whole Book of the Law aloud to them."},{"sc":"temple","n":"Then he stood by a pillar and made a covenant before the Lord: to follow God and keep His commands with all his heart and soul. And all the people joined in."},{"sc":"ruinsfire","n":"Josiah cleaned out the whole land. He burned the idols, destroyed the altars to Baal and the stars, and even tore down Jeroboam's altar at Bethel, just as the man of God had foretold 300 years before, naming Josiah!"},{"sc":"temple","n":"Then he held a Passover celebration like none since the days of the judges."},{"sc":"temple","n":"The Bible says: \"Before him there was no king like him, who turned to the Lord with all his heart, all his soul and all his strength. And after him, none arose like him.\""}],"lesson":{"title":"A young leader can turn a nation","text":"Josiah was young, but when he found the truth, he acted on it completely: reading God's word to everyone, making a public promise, and clearing out every idol. One wholehearted young leader can turn many people back to God."},"order":["Josiah gathers all the people","He reads the Book of the Law aloud","He makes a covenant by a pillar","He destroys the idols","He tears down the altar at Bethel","He holds a great Passover"],"quiz":[{"q":"What did Josiah read to all the people?","o":["The Book of the Law","A letter","A poem","A list of taxes"],"why":"2 Kings 23:2."},{"q":"Which old prophecy came true about Josiah?","o":["He would tear down the altar at Bethel","He would build a ship","He would find gold","He would live forever"],"why":"1 Kings 13:2."},{"q":"How did Josiah turn to the Lord?","o":["With all his heart, soul and strength","Halfway","Only on Sundays","Secretly"],"why":"2 Kings 23:25."}],"words":["COVENANT","PASSOVER","JOSIAH","WHOLE","HEART","REFORM"]},"m2dd":{"passage":[[24,10,16],[25,1,12]],"intro":[{"sc":"assyrian","n":"After Josiah, the kings of Judah went back to doing evil. Now a new superpower rose: Babylon, ruled by King Nebuchadnezzar."},{"sc":"assyrian","n":"Nebuchadnezzar besieged Jerusalem. He carried off King Jehoiachin, the royal family, the temple treasures, and 10,000 skilled people to Babylon. (Among the captives was a young man named Daniel.)"},{"sc":"throne","n":"Nebuchadnezzar made Zedekiah king. But Zedekiah rebelled, and the Babylonian army came back and surrounded Jerusalem for a year and a half. Famine gripped the city."},{"sc":"ruinsfire","n":"The walls were broken through. Zedekiah tried to escape by night, but was caught. The Babylonians killed his sons in front of him, then put out his eyes and took him to Babylon in chains."},{"sc":"ruinsfire","n":"They burned God's temple, the king's palace and every great house in Jerusalem, broke down the walls, and carried the people away. Only the poorest were left to farm the land."},{"sc":"ruinsfire","n":"It was exactly what God had warned through prophet after prophet, for hundreds of years."}],"lesson":{"title":"God's warnings are true, and so is His mercy","text":"This is one of the saddest moments in the Bible. God warned patiently for centuries. But a nation that kept rejecting Him finally lost everything. Yet even in the ashes, prophets like Jeremiah wrote: 'His mercies are new every morning.' Leaders take God's warnings seriously, and trust His mercy."},"order":["Babylon rises to power","Nebuchadnezzar takes Jehoiachin and 10,000 captives","Zedekiah rebels","Jerusalem is besieged for a year and a half","The temple is burned","The people are carried to Babylon"],"quiz":[{"q":"Which empire destroyed Jerusalem?","o":["Babylon","Assyria","Egypt","Rome"],"why":"2 Kings 25:1."},{"q":"Who was the king of Babylon?","o":["Nebuchadnezzar","Sennacherib","Pharaoh","Hiram"],"why":"2 Kings 25:1."},{"q":"What happened to God's temple?","o":["It was burned","It was repaired","It was moved","It was sold"],"why":"2 Kings 25:9."}],"words":["BABYLON","JERUSALEM","TEMPLE","EXILE","KING","MERCY"]},"m2ee":{"passage":[[25,27,30]],"intro":[{"sc":"prison","n":"King Jehoiachin of Judah had been a prisoner in Babylon for 37 long years."},{"sc":"prison","n":"Then a new king came to the throne in Babylon: Evil-merodach. In his very first year, he did something unexpected."},{"sc":"throne","n":"He released Jehoiachin from prison! He spoke kindly to him, and gave him a seat of honour above all the other captive kings in Babylon."},{"sc":"throne","n":"Jehoiachin took off his prison clothes. And for the rest of his life, he ate every day at the king's table, with an allowance for all his needs."},{"sc":"throne","n":"That's how 2 Kings ends: not in total darkness, but with a small light of hope. David's family line was still alive. God's promise to David hadn't failed, and from that line, one day, Jesus would come."}],"lesson":{"title":"Hope in the darkest places","text":"After all the destruction, the book ends with a prisoner set free, given new clothes and a seat at the king's table. It's a whisper of hope: God hasn't forgotten His promises. Even in the darkest times, leaders look for, and point to, God's light."},"order":["Jehoiachin is a prisoner for 37 years","A new king rules Babylon","Jehoiachin is released","He is given a seat of honour","He takes off his prison clothes","He eats at the king's table every day"],"quiz":[{"q":"How long was Jehoiachin in prison?","o":["37 years","7 years","1 year","70 years"],"why":"2 Kings 25:27."},{"q":"What did he take off?","o":["His prison clothes","His crown","His sandals","His chains only"],"why":"2 Kings 25:29."},{"q":"Why is this ending hopeful?","o":["David's family line was still alive, and God's promise hadn't failed","Jerusalem was rebuilt","Everyone went home","Babylon fell"],"why":"God keeps His promises."}],"words":["PRISON","FREE","TABLE","HOPE","PROMISE","DAVID"]}});
